    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d746">

                <pb facs="0817" n="805" sameAs="frus:frus1940v03/pg805"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.283H3/126: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Casablanca</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Goold</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Casablanca</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-07-10T15:00:00+01:00">July 10, 1940—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 10—1:58 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>67. In the event of war between Great Britain and France for which preparations
                    are being made, our two British clerks are apprehensive of internment. I shall
                    resist such a measure on the ground of our extraterritoriality in accordance
                    with the views of Mr. Blake.<anchor n="38" xml:id="d746-f1"/> But I should
                    appreciate instructions.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Goold</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d746-f1" n="38" xml:id="d746-f1-content">
                <p>Maxwell Blake, Diplomatic Agent and Consul General at Tangier.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
